I’ll inspect the diffraction data and metadata first, then identify candidate phases, refine a multiphase GSAS-II model, and validate that the exported CIFs, fractions, profile, statistics, and `final.gpx` all come from the same final refinement.

The chemistry strongly suggests lead tungstate as the main reaction product, but the precursor set and 500 °C treatment make residual tungsten/lead oxides plausible. I’m now extracting peak positions and checking the installed phase-identification/refinement interfaces before choosing structures.

The peak set separates into two recognizable families: a scheelite-like series led by the very intense 27.81° line, and the characteristic triplet-rich pattern of monoclinic WO₃ near 23–25° and 33–35°. I’m testing these against full CIF simulations, along with all chemically plausible lead oxides, so that minor residual phases are not inferred from chemistry alone.

The simulated peak positions confirm two crystalline phases after a common ≈+0.42° 2θ offset: scheelite PbWO₄ (I4₁/a) accounts for the 27.81°, 30.02°, 33.16°, 45.13°, 47.37°, 51.59°, and high-angle series; monoclinic WO₃ (P2₁/n) accounts for the 23.53/24.02/24.84° triplet and the 33.66/34.05/34.60° cluster. I’m setting up the quantitative refinement now, including the actual Cu Kα₁/Kα₂ wavelengths and ratio from the metadata.

The first quantitative pass has converged to roughly 70 wt% PbWO₄ and 30 wt% WO₃, with Rwp near 9.2%. I’m tightening the profile model now; the unconstrained generic width terms became highly correlated, so I’m checking physically stable alternatives and residual peaks before accepting fractions or uncertainty estimates.

A constrained profile comparison is behaving much better: fixing the overall histogram scale removes the prior singularity, and phase-specific crystallite broadening gives physical sizes (about 30 nm PbWO₄ and 80–90 nm WO₃) with no negative width terms. The phase fraction is stable near 73.5/26.5 wt%; I’m completing polymorph and profile-sensitivity checks before finalizing.

The residual audit found a real third-phase signature, not just profile error: the unexplained 26.812° peak and companion lines at 14.67°, 31.22°, 32.55°, 50.25°, and 52.52° match residual precursor Pb₃O₄ after the same zero offset. I’m adding and quantifying that phase now, then will recheck whether any peaks remain chemically coherent enough to justify another phase.

The three-phase model lowers Rwp from 8.45% to 5.46% and quantifies Pb₃O₄ at about 16 wt%, confirming it decisively. One dominant residual line remains at 29.55°; its full companion series (30.82°, 38.25°, 45.57°, 49.78°, 53.70°) identifies orthorhombic PbO (massicot), consistent with Pb₃O₄ decomposition near 500 °C. I’m testing that fourth phase against the tetragonal PbO alternative.

Orthorhombic PbO is favored over litharge, but its first trial was numerically unstable because cell, scale, and nanoscale broadening were released together. I’m rebuilding the four-phase refinement in a staged sequence so the PbO cell and fraction are not artifacts of a boundary-hit profile parameter.

The four-phase solution is converged. I’m tightening the deliverables now: trimming `fit.csv` to the profile actually evaluated by GSAS-II, adding provenance/cell details and χ² to `result.json`, then documenting the identification evidence and systematic limits in `report.md`.

The report now separates the formal least-squares precision from the more realistic systematic uncertainty. I’m regenerating every submitted CIF, `fit.csv`, and `result.json` directly from the serialized `final.gpx`, so the tabulated outputs cannot drift from the project.

The cross-check passes: all four fractions sum exactly to 1, the profile contains 8,284 evaluated points with `difference = observed − calculated`, and the reopened project reproduces Rwp = 4.3461%. I’m doing one final structural/CIF integrity check and reviewing the metadata wording before handoff.

Completed the four-phase GSAS-II refinement.

| Crystalline phase | Weight fraction |
|---|---:|
